 Q2) What is Encapsulation?
 (Encapsulation = Data ko protect karna + us data ko access karne ka controlled way dena.)

 Encapsulation is the process of wrapping data (variables) and methods that operate on that data into a single class, while restricting direct access to the data.


 ***************************"Give me a real-life example."

 A bank account is a good example. The account balance should not be directly accessible or modified. We keep the balance private and provide methods like deposit() and withdraw() to control how the balance is changed.

*****************In Java, encapsulation is mainly achieved by:
1) Declaring variables as private
2) Providing public getter and setter methods

 class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age > 0) {
            this.age = age;
        }
    }
}

Now you cannot directly access name or age from outside the class:

Student s = new Student();

s.setName("Rahul");
s.setAge(20);

System.out.println(s.getName());
System.out.println(s.getAge());


