Q3) Why use encapsulation? 

1) Data security — prevents direct modification of data.
2) Control — you can validate data before changing it.
3) Data hiding — users interact with the class through controlled methods.

 In encapsulation, control and validation are two important benefits.


1. Control (Methods ke through decide kar sakte hain ki data kaise change hoga.)
Encapsulation gives us control over how data is accessed or modified.

For example, if age is public:

class Student {
    public int age;
}

Anyone can do:

Student s = new Student();
s.age = -50;

There is no control over what value gets assigned.

With encapsulation:

class Student {
    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

Now the outside world cannot directly change age. It must go through setAge().



2. Validation
Because we control access through methods, we can validate the data before storing it.

class Student {
    private int age;

    public void setAge(int age) {
        if (age >= 1 && age <= 100) {
            this.age = age;
        } else {
            System.out.println("Invalid age");
        }
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }
}

Now:

Student s = new Student();

s.setAge(20);    // Valid
s.setAge(-10);   // Invalid
s.setAge(150);   // Invalid



 encapsulation is not just about "hiding data"; it's also about controlling and protecting the data.


