Q7) Hybrid Inheritance : (Hybrid = multiple inheritance patterns ka combination.)


Hybrid inheritance is a combination of two or more types of inheritance, such as hierarchical and multiple inheritance. Java does not support hybrid inheritance directly through classes because it does not support multiple inheritance of classes. However, interfaces can be used to model multiple capabilities and complex type relationships.

       A
      / \
     B   C
      \ /
       D
*******************
class A {}

class B extends A {}

class C extends A {}

// ❌ Java does not allow:
// class D extends B, C

*************************************Example :

interface Camera {

    void takePhoto();                    // ❌ yaha implementation nahi
}

interface GPS {

    void navigate();
}

class Smartphone implements Camera, GPS {

    public void takePhoto() {                               // ✅ actual implementation
        System.out.println("Taking photo");
    }

    public void navigate() {
        System.out.println("Navigating using GPS");
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Smartphone phone = new Smartphone();

        phone.takePhoto();
        phone.navigate();
    }
}

*************************************Output:

Taking photo
Navigating using GPS


(An interface defines a contract. It declares what a class must do, while the implementing class provides the actual implementation. A class can implement multiple interfaces, which allows Java to achieve multiple inheritance of type.)


