Q12)  Overloading vs Overriding

| Point        | Overloading                         | Overriding                        |
| ------------ | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Meaning      | Same name, different parameters     | Child provides new implementation |
| Classes      | Usually same class                  | Parent + Child                    |
| Parameters   | Must be different                   | Same method signature             |
| Inheritance  | Not required                        | Required                          |
| Purpose      | Different inputs                    | Different behavior                |
| Polymorphism | Compile-time                        | Runtime                           |
| Example      | `add(int,int)` / `add(int,int,int)` | `Animal.sound()` / `Dog.sound()`  |


***********Scenario 1

1) "You have a calculator and want add() to work with 2 or 3 numbers. What will you use?"

Method overloading, because the operation is the same but the number of inputs is different.

****************************************************************************

2) "You have a Payment parent class and UPI and Card subclasses. Each payment type needs its own pay() implementation. What will you use?"

Method overriding, because the child classes need different implementations of the same method defined in the parent class.

*****************************************************************************
3) "Does overloading require inheritance?"

No. Method overloading does not require inheritance. It can be performed within the same class.

******************************************************************************

4) "Does overriding require inheritance?"

Yes. Method overriding requires a parent-child relationship, because the child class overrides a method inherited from the parent.

******************************************************************************

5) "Can we overload by changing only return type?"

No. Changing only the return type is not enough for method overloading. The parameter list must be different.

**********************************************************************************

6) "Why is overriding called runtime polymorphism?"

Because when an overridden method is called through a parent reference, the implementation that executes is determined at runtime based on the actual object.




