Q14) "What are the requirements for runtime polymorphism?"

Runtime polymorphism is achieved through method overriding. It requires a parent-child relationship through inheritance, and the child class must provide a method with the same name and parameter list as the parent method. When a parent reference refers to a child object, the overridden method is selected at runtime based on the actual object.


Inheritance + Method Overriding + Parent reference with Child object = Runtime Polymorphism.



