Q1. What is an abstract class?

An abstract class is a class declared with the abstract keyword. It can contain abstract methods as well as concrete methods, fields, and constructors. We cannot create its object directly.

***********************************************

Q2. Can we create an object of an abstract class?

No, we cannot directly instantiate an abstract class. However, we can create a reference of the abstract class that refers to a child-class object.

Example:

Payment p = new UPI(500);

*********************************************

Q3. Can an abstract class have a constructor?

Yes. An abstract class can have a constructor. Its constructor is executed when a child-class object is created.

*****************************************************

Q4. Can an abstract class have normal methods?

Yes. An abstract class can contain both abstract methods and concrete methods.

***************************************************************

Q5. Can an abstract class have fields?

Yes. An abstract class can have instance fields, static fields, final fields, and other members like a normal class.

*******************************************************************

Q6. Is implementing an abstract method compulsory?

Yes, a concrete child class must implement all inherited abstract methods. If it does not, the child class must also be declared abstract.

*****************************************************************

Q7. Why can't we create an object of an abstract class?

Because an abstract class may contain abstract methods without implementation. It represents an incomplete/general concept and is intended to be extended by concrete classes.

******************************************************************

Q8. Can an abstract class have zero abstract methods?

Yes.

Example:

abstract class Vehicle {

    void stop() {
        System.out.println("Stop");
    }
}

Ye valid hai.

***************************************************************

Q9) abstract class me static hota he kya ? aur hum directly access kr skte he kya ?

Yes, an abstract class can have static fields and static methods. Static members belong to the class, so they can be accessed directly using the abstract class name without creating an object.
















